Reject non-positive genome lengths passed to -L.

Apart from Makefile.patch's build-flag fix, this is the only downstream change
in this recipe: everything else is upstream code exactly as released in v0.6.2.
It fixes a defect in the -L implementation that upstream shipped and has not
addressed.

getLong() parsed the -L argument with strtol() and cast the result straight to
uint64_t, so a negative argument wrapped instead of being rejected:

    $ Genrich -t in.sam -o out.np -v -L -1
      Genome length: -1bp

The reported "-1bp" is itself an artefact of printing a uint64_t with %ld; the
value actually stored and used is 18446744073709551615. It feeds
  logN = -log10f(genomeLen);
so the background lambda -- and every q-value derived from it -- is computed
against a genome some 15 orders of magnitude larger than any real one, with no
warning and a zero exit status.

Upstream did add a guard, but it cannot fire:

    if (genomeLen < 0)          /* genomeLen is uint64_t: never true */
      exit(error("", ERRGENLEN));

The comparison is made on the already-converted unsigned value, by which point
the sign is gone, so the test folds away. That check is left in place here,
untouched. Validation is instead done at parse time, where the sign survives.

Behaviour changes, each replacing a previously silent wrong answer:

    -L -1                     was: genome length 2^64-1    now: ERRGENLEN
    -L 0                      was: silently auto-computed  now: ERRGENLEN
    -L 99999999999999999999   was: saturated to LLONG_MAX  now: ERRGENLEN

Non-numeric input is unaffected: -L abc already raised ERRINT and still does.

Two deliberate details. The empty first argument in error("", ERRGENLEN) is
required: unlike ERRINT, the ERRGENLEN message carries no leading ": "
separator, so passing the offending string would run it into the message text.
And strtoll/LLONG_MAX are used rather than errno/ERANGE because Genrich.c
already includes <limits.h> but not <errno.h>, keeping this patch free of new
includes; the LLONG_MAX test rejects a literal 9223372036854775807 along with
genuine overflow, which is harmless for a genome length.

Reported upstream, with the reproduction above, at:
https://github.com/jsh58/Genrich/issues/102#issuecomment-5513399276

v0.6.2 (released 2026-08-30) tags commit da43989 of 2023-05-08, so the current
release ships this defect unchanged. Happy to drop this patch as soon as it is
fixed upstream, or sooner if the maintainers would rather ship upstream's
behaviour unchanged.
diff --git a/Genrich.c b/Genrich.c
index 576586f..dc560a1 100644
--- a/Genrich.c
+++ b/Genrich.c
@@ -127,10 +127,12 @@ int getInt(char* in) {
  */
 uint64_t getLong(char* in) {
   char* endptr;
-  uint64_t ans = (uint64_t) strtol(in, &endptr, 10);
+  long long ans = strtoll(in, &endptr, 10);
   if (*endptr != '\0')
     exit(error(in, ERRINT));
-  return ans;
+  if (ans <= 0 || ans == LLONG_MAX)
+    exit(error("", ERRGENLEN));
+  return (uint64_t) ans;
 }
 
 /* char* getLine()
